{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "\"To access the print version of the article, a link is provided. The rules for solving the Sudoku puzzle are clearly stated, with a twist on the classic game. The numbers in the cells contained within grey lines have a specific sum, which is indicated by the figures in the corner. This unique rule adds a new layer of complexity to the puzzle, requiring careful consideration of each number placement.\n\n\n \nAddition to the standard Sudoku rules, the numbers within each shape formed by grey lines must not be repeated. This means that each shape must contain a unique set of numbers, adding an extra level of challenge to the puzzle. The goal is to fill in the missing numbers while adhering to these rules, making it a fun and engaging puzzle to solve.\n\n\n \nThe puzzle is presented in a way that allows readers to test their problem-solving skills. By following the rules and using logic, readers can attempt to complete the puzzle on their own. The sense of accomplishment that comes with solving the puzzle is rewarding, making it a enjoyable activity for those who enjoy puzzles.\n\n\n \nFor those who are unable to solve the puzzle, the completed solution will be available in next week's Observer Digital Edition. This provides an opportunity for readers to check their work and see the correct solution, allowing them to learn from their mistakes and improve their problem-solving skills. The availability of the solution also adds an extra layer of convenience for readers.\n\n\n \nOverall, the Sudoku puzzle offers a fun and challenging way to exercise one's brain. With its unique rules and engaging gameplay, it is an activity that can be enjoyed by people of all ages and skill levels. Whether you are a seasoned puzzle solver or just looking for a new challenge, the Sudoku puzz